        fp: "A manufacturer of commercial hydraulic elevators installed an elevator in an office building. Due to a defect in the hydraulic fluid pressure relief valve, the elevator car descended uncontrollably from the third floor and crashed into the basement shock springs. A passenger inside suffered a fractured ankle. A building maintenance technician who heard the crash ran down the hallway, opened the elevator shaft door with an emergency key, and reached his hand inside to shut off the main electrical disconnect switch. While reaching for the switch, an exposed, uninsulated 480-volt wire inside the elevator controller—carelessly left exposed by the elevator manufacturer's installation team—delivered an electric shock that caused severe burns and cardiac arrest to the technician. The technician sued the elevator manufacturer under strict products liability.",
        q: "What is the legal effect of the technician's attempt to turn off the electrical disconnect switch?",
        opts: [
            "It constitutes secondary implied assumption of the risk that completely bars recovery under strict products liability.",
            "It operates as an extraordinary superseding cause that cuts off the manufacturer's liability as a matter of law.",
            "It does not bar recovery because under the rescue doctrine, reasonable efforts to mitigate a product-caused emergency to protect property and persons are foreseeable.",
            "It limits the technician to a workers' compensation administrative claim against the building owner."
        ],
        ans: 2,
        exp: "Rule: Under the rescue doctrine and emergency mitigation principles (Restatement (Third) of Torts § 32), an individual who acts in an emergency to protect human life or property from an imminent peril created by a defendant's tortious conduct (a defective product causing an elevator crash) does NOT commit an unforeseeable superseding act or voluntarily assume the risk, provided the intervention is not completely reckless or rash under the emergency circumstances. Rushing to shut off an active electrical or mechanical power source after a major elevator failure is a normal, foreseeable human emergency reaction. The rescue doctrine applies to products liability claims, preserving the manufacturer's proximate liability to the injured rescuer (Option C). Option A is incorrect because emergency interventions to mitigate active casualties are not voluntary assumptions of the risk. Option B is incorrect because emergency responses to product failures are foreseeable, not superseding causes. Option D is incorrect because workers' compensation applies to suits against employers, but does not bar third-party strict products liability actions against equipment manufacturers."
