        fp: "A commercial bakery entered into a written contract with an agricultural commodities supplier to purchase 5,000 bushels of high-protein spring wheat at $6.00 per bushel ($30,000 total), with delivery scheduled for August 1. The contract provided: 'F.O.B. Seller's grain elevator, Omaha. Payment due 30 days after delivery.' On July 25, the supplier delivered 5,000 bushels of wheat to an independent commercial railroad, properly loaded in covered hopper cars with reasonable shipping arrangements, and obtained a negotiable bill of lading. The supplier immediately emailed a copy of the bill of lading and notice of shipment to the bakery. On July 28, while the train was traveling through Iowa, an unprecedented 7.0-magnitude earthquake sheared the rail line, derailing the train and spilling all 5,000 bushels of wheat into a river, destroying the entire shipment without fault by the carrier or the supplier. The bakery refused to pay the $30,000 invoice, asserting that the goods were destroyed before arriving at its commercial facility.",
